🔹 Course Overview

This course focuses on hands-on technical skills required for entry-level cybersecurity roles.
It introduces Linux operating systems, command-line tools, and SQL for investigating security incidents and analyzing data.

This course is critical for roles such as:
Cybersecurity Analyst
SOC Analyst
Blue Team Analyst

✅ Module 1: Introduction to Linux
🔹 What is Linux?
Linux is an open-source operating system widely used in:
Servers
Cloud infrastructure
Security tools (SIEM, firewalls, IDS)
Cybersecurity professionals use Linux for:
Log analysis
System monitoring
Security tool execution

🔹 Linux Architecture (High Level)
Kernel → Core of the OS
Shell → Interface between user and system
File System → Organizes data

🔹 Why Linux is Important in Cybersecurity?
Strong permission model
Powerful command-line tools
Widely used in enterprise environments

✅ Module 2: Linux Command Line Basics
🔹 Navigating the File System
Command	Purpose
pwd	Show current directory
ls	List files
cd	Change directory
🔹 File & Directory Management
Command	Purpose
touch	Create file
mkdir	Create directory
cp	Copy files
mv	Move/rename files
rm	Delete files
🔹 Viewing & Searching Files
Command	Purpose
cat	View file
less	Scroll file
head/tail	View start/end
grep	Search text
🔹 Permissions & Ownership
chmod → Change permissions
chown → Change ownership

Permission types:
Read (r)
Write (w)
Execute (x)

🔹 Example
chmod 600 secure_file.txt
Allows only the owner to read and write the file.

✅ Module 3: Linux for Security Operations
🔹 Process & System Monitoring
Command	Purpose
ps	View processes
top	Real-time monitoring
who	Logged-in users
netstat / ss	Network connections

🔹 Log Analysis
Common log files:
/var/log/auth.log
/var/log/syslog

Example:
grep "failed" /var/log/auth.log
Used to detect failed login attempts.

🔹 Why Logs Matter in Security?
Logs help in:
Incident detection
Forensics
Compliance audits

✅ Module 4: Introduction to SQL
🔹 What is SQL?
SQL (Structured Query Language) is used to:
Store data
Retrieve data
Analyze security events
Security teams use SQL to query:
Login records
Alert databases
Incident reports

🔹 Basic SQL Commands
Command	Purpose
SELECT	Retrieve data
FROM	Specify table
WHERE	Filter data
ORDER BY	Sort results
LIMIT	Restrict output
🔹 Example
SELECT username, login_time
FROM login_logs
WHERE status = 'FAILED';

✅ Module 5: SQL for Security Analysis
🔹 Advanced Queries
   Filtering suspicious activity
   Identifying repeated failures
   Correlating events

🔹 Example Use Case
Detect brute-force attempts:
SELECT username, COUNT(*)
FROM login_logs
WHERE status = 'FAILED'
GROUP BY username
HAVING COUNT(*) > 5;

🔹 Why SQL is Important for Cybersecurity?
Used in SIEM tools
Helps in threat detection
Supports incident investigations

